#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/id #uuid "705ea272-efb2-54f4-b527-c1d4305e0c6c" :question/math-academy-id "e-514" :question/problem "Evaluate  $\\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x  \n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t&= \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\int_{a}^0 \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\t\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t& =  \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}\\left( \\arctan (x) \\Big|_a^0 \\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n \t \t\t\t& =  \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan \\left( 0 \\right) -  \\arctan \\left( a \\right) \\right] \\[5pt]\n\n \t\t\t        & =  0 - \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}  \\arctan \\left( a \\right) \\[5pt]\n\n                                &= - \\left(-\\dfrac \\pi 2 \\right)\\[5pt]\n\n \t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function (i.e., it is symmetrical about the $y$-axis). This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^\\infty \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x  =\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x &=   \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x +  \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+ \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\pi.}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/id #uuid "dbd8b706-9ace-5adb-9c8e-29b0d21bfa82" :question/math-academy-id "e-515" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = e^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is convergent\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is divergent\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty, \\infty),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our integrals.\n\n- Computing the integral in statement I, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n&=  \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\int_{0}^{a} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\n&=  \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\left[-e^{-x}\\right]_0^a\\\n&=  -\\lim_{a \\to \\infty}  \\left({e^{-a}}-1\\right)\\\n&=  1.\n}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges, and statement I is true.\n\n- Computing the integral in statement II, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{e}^{-x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-a}-1) \\\\  & =+\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral diverges, and statement II is true.\n\n- The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx= \\int_{-\\infty}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_0^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nHowever, the first integral on the right-hand side is divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent, and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are all true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/id #uuid "aad39658-8d3f-5893-b7dd-603b62064c5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-516" :question/problem "Find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y = \\dfrac{9}{1+4x^2}$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/assets/6c86b60d83c741287c2b7770bfddd67fe9dcfece5ba255edf8ebdb8f0360a975.png)\n\nIn this case, we must calculate the improper integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAs usual, we break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x  + \\displaystyle  \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac {9}{4x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x  & =  9\\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}\\left( \\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\arctan \\left(2x \\right) \\right]_a^0 \\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n \t \t\t\t                                                   & = \\dfrac{9}{2} \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan \\left( 0 \\right) -  \\arctan \\left( 2a \\right) \\right]  \\[5pt] \n\n \t\t\t                                                           & =  0 - \\dfrac{9}{2}\\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}  \\arctan \\left( 2a \\right) \\[5pt] \n\n \t\t\t                                                           & = -\\dfrac{9}{2} \\left(-\\dfrac \\pi 2 \\right)\\[5pt]\n\n \t\t\t\t                                                   & = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}.\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. Therefore, we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^\\infty\\dfrac {9}{4x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x =  \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}+ \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}= \\dfrac{9\\pi}{2} .\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the required area is equal to $\\dfrac{9\\pi}{2}$ square units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9525ff3-b890-517b-8dad-627b0bf61f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310234} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe02fbb-717f-560e-9a57-5e89e7e518a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310235} {:answer/id #uuid "1e62f997-621e-5f31-92ee-f09f1712c43a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310236} {:answer/id #uuid "92a14a43-27a0-5b05-b729-5efd19f70545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310237} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ddacee-a24c-5edb-9c13-f741366d2db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfe02fbb-717f-560e-9a57-5e89e7e518a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310235} :answer-field/id #uuid "14053664-c185-5448-ab52-b588172e5b3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9731bfc9-4d0e-5e7d-ad16-7fdb4db34101" :question/math-academy-id "q-118755" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}+4$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a834425c-6e01-5a0b-97f6-42d2d3a5cc7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310241} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb09c0f-2b77-5fc7-b27a-31678ad9d0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310242} {:answer/id #uuid "6c93a9b8-2f98-528b-becc-6b3127cb48e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310243} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdc7c12-5364-5ded-a490-36055d64d309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310244} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c516d6-c2ec-5844-8a51-c15ba96f6595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a834425c-6e01-5a0b-97f6-42d2d3a5cc7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310241} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a4c39c-e07c-5819-9400-645250c66d8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "41dd8627-ea97-565b-923e-1d48b30365be" :question/math-academy-id "q-118761" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd3ade0c-f13d-57a4-9d07-ea322b62880c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310248} {:answer/id #uuid "818acab4-0c5e-5cc0-b704-ace596e35ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310249} {:answer/id #uuid "36bc91f6-b388-527f-99a7-434826a36dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310250} {:answer/id #uuid "39f3c94d-c7e0-5561-9206-8caacd21cde9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310251} {:answer/id #uuid "47a7d499-b2c3-5de1-afcc-6c97d1f5a724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f3c94d-c7e0-5561-9206-8caacd21cde9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310251} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4577ff1-22f1-59f5-8a6d-1d3b13e1d80a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdb54a64-93e2-5932-b064-6b9249a80c54" :question/math-academy-id "q-118774" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-{e}^{-{x}^{2}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}-(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}})) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9987275b-7b4e-51bb-87db-2a7968de536a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310255} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5747d7-3bdd-5bac-95a7-35f751d96199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310256} {:answer/id #uuid "d5372f1e-1b77-53a7-a560-0a3206df503b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310257} {:answer/id #uuid "9e430db5-2767-5d0c-a490-f4b02327f9a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310258} {:answer/id #uuid "870000f7-585e-5567-94e4-de7fbef647b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9987275b-7b4e-51bb-87db-2a7968de536a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310255} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6a829d3-5612-57d9-bd6f-4d8180fa7b2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80c6d86f-8435-5495-bf6a-386500917e58" :question/math-academy-id "q-118860" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{4}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{4}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+\\frac{3\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e49169e1-b144-5b2a-887c-6c82843c0968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310262} {:answer/id #uuid "9a804290-a1ca-540d-beea-18594005b9c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310263} {:answer/id #uuid "5af85ef8-30cb-5476-8eec-9eb41318029f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310264} {:answer/id #uuid "985077f4-ed3f-5cf5-8805-91514c6ac2d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310265} {:answer/id #uuid "de02a487-8618-5251-8608-4e0612edf82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de02a487-8618-5251-8608-4e0612edf82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310266} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0e62e8d-16b3-5e7f-b976-02a12c5613da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a9eee35-dbac-5e55-af31-1501e52f8af5" :question/math-academy-id "q-20550" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{4}$,  then $\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{-{x}^{4}}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{-{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{-{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4223f18-ee72-587f-989c-41030a43d6b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310269} {:answer/id #uuid "33c207f3-3182-5911-bcc8-b65d2c325697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310270} {:answer/id #uuid "0c16fe42-e474-5edc-896e-96ad0e1ade62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310271} {:answer/id #uuid "b305191b-6cfe-52e3-923c-59f50f9ecc90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310272} {:answer/id #uuid "c70e0bf6-7a5a-5bec-a48d-c097081694ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33c207f3-3182-5911-bcc8-b65d2c325697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310270} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fb0181c-1584-5760-88de-bfebcb5b0fb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "203653d0-31e1-5f6d-9f7e-60818b70d4a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-20788" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f03243d9-7f88-584e-9dfa-3543ff05667b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f03243d9-7f88-584e-9dfa-3543ff05667b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310276} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf7c472-20b8-5915-a5ec-6ab64665a6ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f11bbf3c-0e03-5f1b-aaec-a7531138e083" :question/math-academy-id "q-347621" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=5+{x}^{8}$,  then $\\text{d}u=8{x}^{7}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5+0}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a11f60-e105-5740-86e4-a2975d434eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310279} {:answer/id #uuid "d86c66e2-f75b-5fa9-a320-d886faeb1b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d86c66e2-f75b-5fa9-a320-d886faeb1b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310280} :answer-field/id #uuid "91a96c93-d73c-5bc4-8e2b-bc2ac797af2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2f8f361-f29d-5553-86f4-da64e37065d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-351545" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot [0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7f7daef-d6f6-50cb-bf96-f325761c3d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7f7daef-d6f6-50cb-bf96-f325761c3d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310283} :answer-field/id #uuid "f87090dc-26b0-5918-ac81-25a73de8940d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7684eba-89eb-5863-8f99-8145b16ea602" :question/math-academy-id "q-351585" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=2{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=4x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{0}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e31e15c1-c874-5187-88e3-a48314f91aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e31e15c1-c874-5187-88e3-a48314f91aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310286} :answer-field/id #uuid "68e7cacb-cbcb-5a8d-b15f-69d052782912" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b35850a-9c2e-5107-b377-a172e63b7cb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-351595" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/assets/ccd0312a25669fec949475eaadb042e0c660d094c7579f5bc4f98b68fc9be78d.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}){|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{16}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{16}+\\frac{3\\pi }{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfd690c3-c823-518a-8ea9-bd14623d30c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfd690c3-c823-518a-8ea9-bd14623d30c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310289} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba779321-fc86-57fb-be6e-6ca842951e91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f31187e3-7cbb-506d-af11-b921e767e7f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-351600" :question/problem "Find the total area $A$ of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}$ and the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/assets/c2104caf050dd3bb795f29b40ce3047e7c8cea98f7c0a6abede5f92c23a06815.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={e}^{2x}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{1+({e}^{2x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6aa286e-1b6c-51e6-a6f4-e68019b4bac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104346} {:answer/id #uuid "d97626ea-6f0e-55f2-b209-0383321a073c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104347} {:answer/id #uuid "da9f020d-dc73-5b69-9701-d5f631fba492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104348} {:answer/id #uuid "16729967-8299-58fc-ace8-ed958239489f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104349} {:answer/id #uuid "6c55baf1-e3b7-5c83-abd6-393401fd2f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9f020d-dc73-5b69-9701-d5f631fba492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104348} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7eda9a0-048b-59a3-b01d-6f89e7f2fb99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d136f69-0617-5d12-ad3b-b952611b8b09" :question/math-academy-id "q-51075" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({0}^{3}-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to -\\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eab8e23c-f8ac-5849-baee-03938fa4522d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310292} {:answer/id #uuid "0062029e-fe63-5b21-bc66-8a1fa62fa9a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310293} {:answer/id #uuid "1f740ded-2b09-5901-81a5-0ce42c8c0d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310294} {:answer/id #uuid "93f6f09a-c6c4-5cf4-a922-314dd0599adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310295} {:answer/id #uuid "10561597-0e6f-54cc-92c3-cc7662d0f3dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0062029e-fe63-5b21-bc66-8a1fa62fa9a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310293} :answer-field/id #uuid "4446dbf4-8054-58a4-b632-598eaf9f4443" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f18d536-32a1-5975-855f-f52f46d77aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-6302" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={e}^{x}$,  then $\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function.  Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{{e}^{2x}\\cdot ({e}^{-2x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4e26641-f23c-5311-8eb7-220569b328e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105036} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdf43ef-0130-55bf-8e8c-23a47920f804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105037} {:answer/id #uuid "f38b3b92-ee8e-571f-aee8-fbeceed581c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105038} {:answer/id #uuid "2a00a7c7-b1ef-56af-b305-b1e740f5bc93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105039} {:answer/id #uuid "2cebb526-29d4-531e-b502-894803577248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cebb526-29d4-531e-b502-894803577248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105040} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2ac2ed4-e0ec-5fba-b219-a7b40d091cd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a815879d-3333-5bf0-a2f7-1d630f329248" :question/math-academy-id "q-81168" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/assets/478ea8a880f959248ae2885776ca3c2b3774935f96ddb64ce531adc6fc5b3ef7.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{3}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b367d6b8-dc48-53e1-b99a-059c738fc142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310299} {:answer/id #uuid "5e647f77-36e2-568a-9f6e-2c4a7292ebe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310300} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cb1c9a-e1b9-56dc-920d-a0ae428af52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310301} {:answer/id #uuid "c39062a7-ba40-5f22-88b6-544afff931b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310302} {:answer/id #uuid "6f32a005-0e91-5c7f-bbcd-9ecc46c6c44c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8cb1c9a-e1b9-56dc-920d-a0ae428af52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310301} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9aa03e-61e1-53a8-a84b-78ad52987fc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7604850e-71a3-5707-ae7f-3270a2b4ae23" :question/math-academy-id "q-81375" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}+{e}^{-{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}]}
